[50.468374, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[51.020499, "o", "Issue #1: Phase 1: outstanding items, due 23:59 Friday 30 October 2026\r\nState: OPEN | Author: danvil80 | Created: 2026-10-16\r\n--------------------------------------------------\r\n**Phase 1 is not signed off yet.** Reviewed by Len Viljoen on 2026-10-16 at commit `936b98ee9b10`.\r\r\n\r\r\n## What must change\r\r\n\r\r\nThis feedback is about your repository as it stood on 15 October 2026 (commit 936b98e). Start with the numbered list under What must change before sign-off.\r\r\n\r\r\n## What you did well\r\r\n\r\r\n- Your charter excludes funded or real cloud accounts and sets a budget of R0.00, so nothing you build depends on infrastructure the Academy does not control. See charter/charter.md.\r\r\n- Your scope and objectives are about Nomvula and not a generic company: rural branch workstations, offline sync, data tampering, endpoint theft, a measured recovery time and recovery point, and a pipeline that blocks unencrypted storage, secret leaks and container vulnerabilities.\r\r\n- threat-model/threat-model-v0-phase1.md has exactly three risks, and they are the right ones for a business with remote sites: data exposed when an offline device is stolen, tampering and replay of offline batches when a branch reconnects, and ransomware during planting and harvest windows through phished remote credentials. Each has a scenario and an impact, and you kept to three after your research drifted towards smart-farming hardware.\r\r\n- charter/POPIA-Exposure.md uses scenarios from Nomvula's own work: stolen field laptops, a rural network dropping during a sync, offline tampering, and applications cached on branch devices. Each one is tied to a control.\r\r\n- GIT_RULES.md is a real working protocol. It says never push directly to main, one task and one branch and one person, separate file ownership, pull before you start, three questions to ask each day, and merge through a pull request. Your charter names a Build Lead and an Assurance Lead, and says roles rotate at each phase boundary.\r\r\n- Each of you wrote a reflection that answers the four questions. Lutho's names a real difficulty in the work. Michael's is plain about finding the terminal and the POPIA sources hard.\r\r\n- Lutho's recordings in evidence/lutho/ show Git, the OpenTofu version, the OpenTofu install with its checksum and signature steps, and Docker hello-world. Your pipeline is in place and passing, and your evidence is text only with no screenshots.\r\r\n\r\r\n## What must change before sign-off\r\r\n\r\r\n1. Write the business profile in charter/business-profile.md. That file is empty. The text in charter/business_model.md is a plan to move from on-premises servers to a cloud with Multi-AZ replication, a VPC and a 99.9 percent uptime target. It could describe any financial firm, and it does not fit the zero-cost, emulated, offline-first design in your charter. Write one page about Nomvula itself: its sector and size, its branches and depot desks in remote areas, how poor connectivity shapes the work, the planting and harvest cycles and the loan and insurance demand they bring, and the categories of personal information it holds, such as the identity documents, credit histories, bank records and land deeds named in your POPIA file. Say plainly whether Nomvula holds special personal information as section 26 of POPIA defines it. Ask yourselves, for example, whether insurance claims carry health details or whether members are identified by biometrics. If your answer is no, say why. Give growth pressures as numbers and label them as assumptions. Drop the Multi-AZ, VPC and uptime claims or explain how they fit an offline-first design, and say what happens when a branch is offline for days. Use Markdown headings. The title in charter/business_model.md has no space after the hash mark, so a screen reader does not read it as a heading. When the new profile is in place, delete charter/business_model.md.\r\r\n2. Merge at least one pull request on GitHub, with a review. GIT_RULES.md says to merge through a pull request, but as of 15 October none had been merged. The history shows commits going straight to main, and the one bran"]
[51.020653, "o", "ch you used on 4 September was merged with a local Git command. Create a branch, put fixes from this list on it, open a pull request, let Michael review it as Assurance Lead and leave a comment, then merge it.\r\r\n3. Complete the toolchain evidence in evidence/. Add recordings that show an OpenTofu minimal plan, a bucket created and then listed against Moto, and gitleaks, Checkov and Trivy each producing output. In evidence/lutho/test.cast, Checkov and Moto appear only in a list of installed packages, which shows they are installed and not that they ran. Michael has no recording, so record your own environment too and commit it from your own account. Move evidence/lutho/whois.cast out of evidence/. It records lookups of unrelated public domains and is not toolchain evidence. Rename evidence/lutho/business-profile.cast, which records writing the POPIA file, so that its name says what it shows.\r\r\n4. Correct charter/POPIA-Exposure.md. Information Quality is Condition 5, not Condition 4. The tampering scenario filed under \"Condition 6: Openness & Auditability\" belongs with Condition 7, security safeguards. \"Condition 8: Data Subject Participation & Retention (Condition 3)\" mixes two conditions: retention comes under purpose specification in Condition 3, and data subject participation is Condition 8. The list headed \"Special Personal & Financial Information\" holds bank records, credit histories, asset valuations, land deeds, harvest projections and membership IDs. These are sensitive financial data, but they are not special personal information as section 26 defines it, so rename the list and answer the question in item 1. AES-256 and TLS 1.3 with mutual authentication are shown as mandatory. Section 19 of POPIA requires appropriate, reasonable technical and organisational measures and names no technology, so say that these are the measures you chose and why. Lutho's reflection repeats the Condition 4 label and calls AES-256 and mutual TLS direct legal requirements, so correct it too. Rename the file to charter/popia-exposure.md, as charter/README.md asks.\r\r\n5. Fix GIT_RULES.md and the recording tools. In GIT_RULES.md the first word of rule 1 is corrupted into unreadable characters (it should read \"Never\"), line 31 has stray characters after \"git checkout main\", and rule 4 has a doubled backtick. Reading tools/recording/helpers.sh, I found typing errors (\"2/dev/null\" with the redirect symbol missing, \"%Ym%d\", \"%H:MHis\" and \"${.#%.cast}\") and a test of \"-mode\" against \"-2\" that can never be true, so the option to publish to main never runs. That option adds everything, merges into main and pushes to main, so once the test is fixed it would break your own rule 1. I did not run the script. Fix it and test it, or remove it. tools/recording/readme.md contains garbled characters and emoji, so replace them with plain text.\r\r\n6. Clean up charter/charter.md. Remove the template's persona-name placeholder from the title and put the hash mark back, so that the title is a Markdown heading and a screen reader announces it. Remove the line \"Complete this in Phase 1.\" and the italic prompts under Scope, Success criteria, Assumptions and Out of scope. Make each success criterion something another person can test. Objective 3 should name the gates and what happens when one fails, for example \"the pipeline fails a run that contains a planted test secret\". Objective 4 should give your recovery time and recovery point figures and say how you measure them. Objectives 2 and 5 should say how someone checks them. The assumption that free-tier runners and local toolchains give \"complete validation parity\" is too strong, because emulators do not behave exactly like a live cloud. Say what you can show and what you can only design.\r\r\n7. Put the threat model where the folder README asks. threat-model/threat-model-v0.md is still the unfilled template, and your model is in threat-model/threat-model-v0-phase1.md. Move your content into threat-model/threat-model-v0.md and delete the other file. Lutho's reflection names threat-model/threat-model-v0.md and char"]
[51.02071, "o", "ter/popia_exposure.md, so make every file name in your reflections match a file that exists. research/threat_model.md is a long general document with more sets of risks and statistics attributed to Accenture, ESET, Snode and Indwe Risk Services, with no links. Either label it as background research and link each source, or remove it, so that anyone reading threat-model/ sees one model with three risks.\r\r\n8. Fix the reflections. Move them into reflections/phase-1-studentA.md (Lutho) and reflections/phase-1-studentB.md (Michael), replacing the unfilled stubs, and delete the reflections/Lutho and reflections/Michael folders. Lutho: your reflection says you collaborated with Michael through GitHub pull requests, reviewing and merging branches. No pull request had been merged yet, so make that true by completing item 2, or change the sentence to say what happened. Michael: your reflection does not mention your Assurance Lead role. Add what you reviewed, naming a file, what you found, and where that is recorded. Also name the files you wrote, such as GIT_RULES.md, the recording tools, the business model and the threat research. Explain what you meant by the threat model \"will expand on this research in the cloud\", given that your charter excludes live cloud deployment.\r\r\n9. Tidy the repository. Delete output.txtsudo, an empty file committed by mistake. In README.md, replace the first names with full names and complete the platform paragraph, which is still the template placeholder. Set your Git name and email so the history shows each person under one identity. Michael's commits use the placeholder address you@heinwagneracademy.org, so GitHub may not link them to Michael's account. Write commit messages that say what changed, because six commits say only \"update\".\r\r\n\r\r\n## What to strengthen in Phase 2\r\r\n\r\r\n- Rank your three risks with a likelihood and an impact, so the model says which one to fix first.\r\r\n- Design the offline sync in detail. Say what happens to a batch of applications after a branch has been offline for three days, and how a replayed or altered batch is rejected or processed only once. Say what you can show on emulated infrastructure and what you can only design.\r\r\n- Decide which of the controls named in charter/POPIA-Exposure.md (Wazuh SIEM, signed audit trails, mutual TLS) you will build and which you will only model, and keep the local AI feature small, so that the scope stays deliverable by two people.\r\r\n- Make the Assurance Lead's work visible. Every merge gets a review comment from the other student on GitHub, and the record stays there.\r\r\n- Commit in small steps across the weeks. There are no commits at all between 4 September and 9 October, so the history does not show the work as it happened.\r\r\n- The starter pipeline in .github/workflows/ci.yml treats the secret scan, Checkov and Trivy as advisory in Phase 1. Read the comments in that file and plan when each one becomes enforcing, because until then a green run does not prove a clean scan.\r\r\n\r\r\n## Questions you must be ready to answer at the panel\r\r\n\r\r\n1. A field laptop has been offline for three days in planting season and is then stolen. What is on it, which control protects the data at each step, and what could you show the panel with OpenTofu, Moto and your pipeline?\r\r\n2. A branch reconnects and uploads the same batch of loan applications twice, or a batch that was altered while it was offline. How does your design detect that, and what would show the panel that the batch was rejected or processed only once?\r\r\n3. Your charter says zero cost, emulated and offline first. How does your view of availability fit that, and does Nomvula hold any special personal information as section 26 of POPIA defines it? Whatever your answer, explain why.\r\r\n\r\r\nWhen the changes are committed and the pipeline is green, comment on this issue so the phase can be checked again.\r\n"]
